Day 12 – Scope and Recursion in Java

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s lecture focused on two important concepts in Java: Scope and Recursion. The mentor explained how variable visibility works within a program and how recursive methods solve problems efficiently.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Scope in Java

Scope defines the visibility and lifetime of variables within a program.

The session covered different types of scope:

✅ Local Variables

Declared inside a method, constructor, or block.

Accessible only within that specific block.

Created when method starts and destroyed when method ends.

✅ Instance Variables

Declared inside a class but outside methods.

Belong to an object (instance) of the class.

Each object has its own copy.

✅ Class-Level (Static) Variables

Declared using static keyword.

Shared among all objects of the class.

Memory allocated only once.

The mentor demonstrated how scope affects variable accessibility and how improper usage can cause errors.

🔹 2️⃣ Recursion in Java

Recursion is a process where a method calls itself to solve a problem.

Key concepts discussed:

Recursive method structure

Importance of a base condition

Preventing infinite recursion

Step-by-step execution flow

The mentor explained how recursion works internally using function calls stored in the call stack.

🔹 Key Points About Recursion

Every recursive method must have a base case.

Recursive calls reduce the problem size step by step.

If the base condition is missing, it can lead to StackOverflowError.

Recursion is commonly used in factorial, Fibonacci, and tree problems.

